Date: Fri, 30 Apr 1993 21:39:10 -0500
From: "Phil G. Fraering" <pgf@srl01.cacs.usl.edu>
Subject: Pueblo Bonito and Paolo Soleri

I've given much thought (and will one day write them down in an essay)
to an insight I had once about Arcosanti while I was reading a book
on Pueblo Bonito and the nearby dwellings...

I will assume all of you know what Pueblo Bonito is (if you don't,
and can't find out by yourself, call or email me).

Not all of you would know that it's sorta like something out of the
Illuminatus! books: it was never inhabited according to the latest
archaeological evidence (i.e. it almost seems like it's a hoax: someone
way back wanted to make it look like there was a village or town there;
"Blazing Saddles" suddenly comes to mind).

But it's located within a network of paths or roads. Out away from
Pueblo Bonito, there were habitations. Literally "wide spots in the
road" where there were three or four (or maybe, getting really lucky)
a dozen habitations grouped together; it's assumed that hunting and
farming was how the inhabitants there supported themselves.

Heck, this life-style goes back way before Pueblo Bonito was built and
abandoned.

I'm wondering if some prehistoric (or might this actually be historic
times, given native american oral histories) Zuni avatar of Paolo
Soleri looked around, got disgusted with all the rural and suburban
lifestyles of the proto-Zunis, and started building (and maybe with
the help of some more dupes completed) with Spock's old "stone knives
and bearskins" a bronze-age version of Arcosanti.

And then, just as now, most people turned their backs on the mad
architect, and stayed out of the hives and firmly entrenched in their
Ranch houses...

Comments?

Phil

