From: Scott C DeLancey <delancey@darkwing.uoregon.edu>
Subject: PUBS: SciAm

On Fri, 30 Apr 1993, Phil G. Fraering wrote:
> 
> Someone talked about how their linguistics was bogus. It might have
> been, but as far as the politics within the linguistic community 
> (going out on a limb here) they'll probably always have "correct"
> articles, tracing linguistic interaction in history by simple
> root words (you know, the standard dozen) and using that as an
> excuse for strict isolationism.

I'm gonna saw off that limb for you.  Apparently one of their editors
has developed an enthusiasm for "long-range" linguistic comparison,
which tries to establish far-reaching linguistic relationships on the
basis of what "correct" historical linguistics would consider insufficient,
dubious, or sometimes outright specious evidence.  This bias was evident
in their summary article on the topic a couple of years ago, and even
more so in their printing the truly egregious Greenberg & Ruhlen article
last fall.  This is definitely NOT "correct" as far as the politics of
the linguistic community is concerned, and for good reason.  (I'm
regarded in really orthodox circles as a bit of a long ranger myself,
and I'm inclined to look sympathetically at serious work in that 
direction that the "correct" folks would dismiss out of hand, but 
Greenberg's stuff is way out to lunch, and his SA article is maybe
the worst thing he's ever done).  Likewise the Renfrew and Gamilkredze/
Ivanov articles espouse fringy theories that few orthodox linguists
take seriously.  (The Gamilkredze stuff is very plausible but still
unproven; the hypothesis Renfrew is selling is simply impossible).

Scott DeLancey



