Date: Fri, 30 Apr 1993 22:15:01 -0400 (EDT)
From: Harry Shapiro <habs@Panix.Com>
Subject: Meta: Judgment Metzger vs. Moore

Introduction/Subject: 
This is a formal presentation of judgment against Ms. Carol Moore, who
was charged with flaming by Mr. Perry Metzger.

I have deliberated to determine if in the message Mr. Metzger cited as
evidence, Ms. Moore, as defined by our list rules, and the common
practices of the list and based upon the precedence of past list
judgments, has flamed.

Summary: 
I find that Carol violated three list rules and I give her a warning
for each violation. I also recommend to her that she try to increase
her reputational valuation by posting less and making higher quality
posts. In this document, I spend a lot of time discussing the issue of
"equal treatment under the law."

The complete decision will be presented at the end of this long and I
fear verbose document.

As side issue, I feel that measures must be made to change this list
so that we can either have members of equal value or a legal code that
can vary according to a members value. Even a good beings reputation
may vary over time, and I don't desire to screen our members
continuously. I look to market forces to set the value for each of us.
I also hope that the list software being re-written my Ray Cromwell
and those who have agreed to help him, will solve this problem by
allowing any and all members to selectively decide to not receive
posts from selected list members. This programming effort, when
finished will also allow logical sub lists to be constructed
algorithmically, agorically or subjectively and will result in
sub-communities of focused interested and closely shared opinions of
value.

Judgment: 
While some members of the list have objected to my use of narrow
points of law to make these judgments, I find such methods useful and
in this case continue that practice. With our list code so un tried
and our code in flux (It is being rewritten as we speak), broad-based
judgments on my part will yield a form of justice that is subjective
at best, leaves little record as to why a decision has been made, and
perhaps limits grounds for appeal. While their is a risk that such
judgments, in the scope of their narrowness, will be seen as providing
detailed parameters for how one should think, and thus, when one is
found guilty by this manner, one might feel one is guilty of a thought
crime; such is not my intent.

We hold that all folks are not equal (while I myself feel that all
conscious beings, at the moment of birth/creation are equal in
potential), and in general practice we hold that each person should be
judged by the value they bring to the community. Yet, the practice of
law is marked by a common principle of equal treatment under the law.

As a warning to those who plan to live in a PPL society, be prepared
to accept no one into your community who, in regards to treatment
under the law, you would not consider your equal; or have some market
function by which, as a function there of, the law can bend and sway,
objectively.

I have received numerous messages from and had conversations with,
list members who feel that Ms. Carol Moore is not valued as a list
member and as a member of this community. Their pleasure would be
that, for even a minor infraction, that Ms. Moore be given the most
harsh punishment I can deliver which is exile from our realm. I should
add that I consider all of these list members to be among the most
valuable members of this community and they rank highest among those
whose opinions, other than mine, which I value greatly.  While I
recall no such direct communications, the gist of at least several of
these was they didn't desire to stay here if Ms.  Moore remains. While
I don't consider that anyone or any group of valued list members will
leave because of this judgment, there is a clear and present danger to
this community: the combination of both open enrollment and equal
treatment under the law may result in a membership whose intellectual
and social "fabric" is far less than ideal.

Thus, I will declare with some regret, that this noble experiment
(largely of my desire and design) to use this mailing list as a test
of a PPL society has not yielded results that I enjoy.  That the
results show that even though the list is private, and that its
control and ownership are private we are still suffering from a
failure of the commons. We cannot have varied treatment under the law
unless we have non-subjective data about each person's value and
during that period of time when this information doesn't exist, our
legal system is a Extropian "failure."

I could however, take an other tact, and say that as a preponderance
of the evidence about Ms. Moore's value presented to me indicates that
Ms. Moore is not a valued list member. That would allow me to, if I
find her guilty, to remove her from the list and declare our code and
this experiment to work. Such a declaration, in my view would be
false.

Despite a clear and common voice from some quarters as to Ms. Moore's
value here, I cannot but find that these opinions are subjective,
taken from a sample of members in a manner that is not statistically
valid, and which has not been set through market forces. Still I find
that it is likely that these valuations of Ms. Moore are by-in-large
well supported by the list members. If it is true that Ms. Moore has a
lower value then our most valued members, is it some what less or
greatly less or some other fraction of lesser value? And then what is
the threshold level of valuation for certain kinds of flaming?  For
example, would a future judgment read "You are a member of lesser
value and have not referred to members of great and greater value as
either Sir or Madam and thus I find that you have flamed." I would
have no issue in making judgments depicted in this admitably silly
example, if it was legally codified and a trusted market method of
setting the valuation existed. I need to add, for other list members
for whom I have a higher level of confidence in their valuation, I
might be willing to put all these issues a side, and make a judgment
based on value, inferring a legal codification into the Extropian
principles, list rules and other official ExI communications.

In the letter cited by Mr. Metzger, which Ms. Moore has either
acknowledged sending or not denied communicating to the list, the
following text has been extracted:

> "Also, it is also intellectually dishonest of you to, for > example,
state that because I state I'm happy the Irish don't > continually
harp on the potato famine, I am telling the Jews to shut > up about
the holocaust.

> Similarly, it is intellectually dishonest of you to assert that >
because I merely speculate about UFO and time travel and reincarnation
> that I must believe all manner of new age hokum.""

Ms. Moore is directing these comments at Mr. May.

By the list rules she would be guilty of flaming if this extract or
any other part of the message, either directly or though clear meta
communication did any of the following:

* Started or continued a fight * Insulted someone * continue a fight
once one has started * responding to "up hold your honor" * responding
to "set the facts straight" * once a fight has started, posts with an
angry tone or voice

The list rules require someone, once a fight has broken out to file a
complaint, and not take the law into their own hands.

1) I judge that based on the message sent by Mr. Metzger,
   that Mr. May and Ms. Moore have been fighting, as defined by the
   list rules, over Mr. May's reputational value of Ms. Moore.

1a) I confirm this judgment though a non-exhaustive reading of list
    traffic and private communications.

2) I find that Ms. Moore, in this post, was replying after a fight
   had broken out "trying to set the record straight."

3) I find that Ms. Moore, in this post, was replying after a fight
   had broken out "trying to up hold her honor."

4) I find that Ms. Moore was, in her usage of the statement,
   "intellectually dishonest of you" was insulting.

I offer no narrow details as to why I judge items 1-3 to be so, as
this seems, to me, clear from the evidence and from, as indicated, my
research.

As for item 4. It is questionable if Mr. May was offended. Since it
was Mr. Metzger, not Mr. May who made the charges.

I note that Mr. May has been called upon by members of the list to act
based upon his reputation for intellectual honesty.

I have also had private communications with Mr. May in which he
indicated he had no intention of bring charges against list members,
so his not filing of charges doesn't mean he was not offended.

I also feel that the list rules do not restrict my judgments to
determine if Mr. May found such words offensive. That the list rules
will allow me to determine if a valued list member or even a average
list member would find Ms. Moore's comments insulting.

I find that an average list member would find such remarks offensive,
if such remarks where made to them and they had the same reputation as
Mr. May.

I wish to point out that this judgment reflects the specific value and
reputation of Mr. May and that such a statement, as made by Ms. Moore,
directed at an other list member may not have resulted in the same
judgment:

Items 2-4 are actionable. I find that Ms. Moore is guilty and give her
a warning for each of these offenses.

That is Ms. Moore has been given 3 out of 3 possible warnings and any
further violations by her will result in removal from the list.

I also recommend, but do not compel, that Ms. Moore make efforts to
make improvements to her reputational valuation. Such efforts should
not be made through the posting of many messages. Ms. Moore should
consider making far far fewer posts to this list than she has made in
the past; each post should be of the highest quality well above the
average S/N ratio. I feel that only in this way will her reputation
improve.


