From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: PHIL: GS + Objectivism (+ Extropianism) = ?

Ray Cromwell:
> I'm not saying you can't measure entropy
> or negentropy, I'm saying you can't state 'minimum entropy' or
> 'maximum negentropy' in something as complex as a social system
> consisting of billions of humans. 

We can certainly measure gross thermodynamic entropy, for starters.
We can create benchmarks that approximate negentropy, some of which
I've suggested.  I'm not saying it's easy, but the alternative is 
to treat negentropy as subjective, which it's not.  We need to measure 
relative entropy, eg "The Mormon religion is less entropic than the
Episcopalian".  We find good nearby solutions, not global minima,
which are computationally intractable in this search space.

I've seen ideas called "entropic" on this list based, not on
any sort of observation related to organization or physics or information
theory, but just because the poster disagreed with the idea ideologically.  
I think that's silly.

> The Japanese seem have succeeded in setting
> up a social system where loyalty and company spirit work, but to the
> detriment of the individual. The Japanese work some of the hardest
> schedules in the world, a lot of workers die or get sick from overwork.
> And suicide is quite normal.

"Here we go again."  This is very similar to the comment made about
irrational Mormons and their risky driving behavior.  The *empirical
facts* here are that Mormons have life expectancies higher than most
other U.S. religions or U.S. agnostics, and Japanese workers have life
exptectancies higher than U.S. workers.   Furthermore, *memetic*
entropy is at least as important as individual entropy.  Mormons
win there, too (intrinsic & total memetic growth), and Japanese win
here in economic terms (vastly outstripping U.S. economic growth
rate since WWII).

Ideology-driven, anti-empirical statements, if widely propagated,
may themselves by highly entropic.

> I hereby denounce negentropy as a goal.

Well then, you have rejected the very basis of Extropianism.
I, on the other hand, embrace extropy but reject some of the
intellectual baggage that has been arbitrarily tacked on it;
specifically Nietchze/Rand individualism.  Although I admire
Nietchze as personally inspiring, I find him nowehere near an
adequate guide to interpersonal relations.  The empirical evidence
of Rand's impact on interpersonal relations seems fairly clear
just from the numerous anecdotes and my own personal experiences;
I suspect the divorce statistics for example are spectacular.  (As ever, 
I stand ready to have these claims empirically verified or falsified).

>I don't think anyone here has condemned the family or small communities.

Not condemned, just grossly devalued.  I'd point to F.M. Esfandiary
as the extreme; he explicitly praises the breakup of families as
an important part of transhumanism.  I find many of F.M. Esfandiary's 
ideas to be highly entropic, perhaps even moreso than Rand's, in the
measurable sense of destroying human negentropic social relationships
without building superior replacements.

> Extropianism argues for a society free of coercion. Individuals
> are free to make firms of any size, including self-employment.

But this describes only one dimension of the human experience (and
probably only one dimension of transhuman and posthuman experience
as well).  It doesn't account for families, religions, art, much
of science, etc.  It only begins to account for law; the 
Friedman/Hanson/et. al. economic analysis of law is highly immature and 
for the most part untested.  I find the extension of markets into other 
aspects of life highly attractive; what is very entropic is the use of
such preliminary constructions to devalue and ignore the vast traditional 
negentropic structures, often implicit or emerged in the form of
subjective, irrational, motivating symbiotic memes, built up over 
thousands of years of civilization.   A central rule of thumb of
good physicians is "first, do no harm"; an important rule for engineers
is "keep the pieces".  It is highly entropic to go around 
bashing other people's negentropic structures because of ideological
differences, which I've seen happen on this list (I even plead guilty
myself).  This was the fundamental mistake of the Saint-Simionans:
rejecting and calling for the destruction of current social structures
without studying them, understanding them, or designing working
replacements.

>    I don't understand what you're arguing unless you think
> elimination of self-employed workers or mass-industrial collectivism
> will lead to a decrease in negentropy.

No, on this front I'm arguing that the converse is not true either.
There are economic niches for both 100,000-person and 1-person
companies, and between, of many varieties.  

> A 
> collectivist will put the values of the group above his own personal needs or
> wishs. An individualist generally will not.

No, a collectivist (in the sense of an extropic, voluntary collective)
typically has personal needs and wishes that correlate highly with 
the needs and wishes of the group.  The Libertarian Party, for
example, is such a collective, and the people who work hard for
the Libertarian cause like Perry Metzger are highly altruistic,
sacrificing their own extropy (longevity, wealth, etc.) for the
task of spreading the meme.  Contrast that with my brother,
who is, like most libertarians, a free rider: absorbed in his
own desires and ambitions, quite uninvolved in politics.
My brother is a true Randian/Nietchzian, while Libertarian
activists often preach Rand but practice the opposite.  (There
are also many anti-Randian Libertarians, like myself).

>    I still don't see how this factors out individualism. The only
> thing I am arguing with is the notion that you could run a large
> corporation based on trust/brother love/altruism alone. 

Not alone, but it often helps, as with the Japanese.  These aspects
are even more important in large groups with non-economic goals, eg
political parties and religions.

The Mormon Church is run almost entirely on these motivations, at
least in principle, and ranks very high in both individual and memetic 
extropian benchmarks I have suggested (longevity, intrinsic & gross 
expansion, etc.) N.B. I am not arguing that the Mormon Church is optimal,  
much less that we should all go out and get baptized, just that it seems 
to be a very powerful storehouse of negentropy that we could learn quite 
a bit from.

Of course if someone comes in with a preconceived ideology, they might
conclude that it is an irrational religion, and therefore entropic, 
and come up with fun anecdotes about crazy Mormon driving habits,
without bothering to check the facts.  The Libertarian Party relies
on very large doses of altruism to function, and the conflict between
this need and the Randian anti-altruist ideology lies at the heart
of the LP's problems (eg the non-involvement of free rider libertarians 
like my brother).

>    How about this empirical fact. Small clone dealers and
> manufacturers have effectly kicked IBM's collective ass in the personal
> computer market. 

That's one market.  In some other markets we're seeing consolidation
and centralization (eg the airlines).  The overall trend may be
towards decentralization, but it's glacial and perhaps temporary.
I have an ideological prejudice towards decentralization just
like you do, it's just that I try not to take trends and ideologies
to the entropic extremes of, eg F.M. Esfandiary (whose methodology
seems to be, "it's the trend, therefore it's good").

> Chrysler, GM, Macdonnell-Douglas, Lockheed, need I say more?

You could say "new stock price highs" for the last two.
Aerospace stocks have been hot lately, at the same time the
industry has been consolidating and centralizing.  There
haven't been any significant new car companies on the planet for 
quite a while, but car sales keeps climbing; overall that's
a major trend towards larger car makers.

>     Huh? Self-interest demands doing a good job and keeping the
> corporation (your bread and butter) in business.

It only "demands" this through a complex set of interactions.  
Any good manager will point out that employee motivation goes
way beyond the abstract numbers on the auto-deposit stub, especially
the day-to-day grind.  Hell, any good hacker will tell you that,
eg Phil Zimmerman.

> Pride, Selfishness, exibitionism, these
> are the motors of individualism. 

But they are not rational, not self-interest, and not necessarily
negentropic for either the individual, or the local group, or the
meme.

> ...On the other hand, systems that I and several others have been
> working on are as easy as hitting a key in elm, and actually
> return a benefit to the user in that he gets a personalized version of
> the list to his preferences.

I look forward to it!  But your claim that your system is easier to
use than HEX is just that, a claim, until we try it out.   Furthermore,
it may need to be orders of magnitude easier than HEX to beat the
functionality of implicit reputation.

>   How do you define "maximum negentropy" and how do you calculate
> whether something will approach it?

The strategy is finding delta-negentropy, ie more or less negentropy 
given the current state as the starting point, not on finding globally 
optimal negentropy.  The various benchmarks I and others have suggested 
provide a good empirical start at observing the relative extropy of memes.
This gives us the ability to search for states of higher extropy.

